---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  The one short document a plan is read and approved as. Its reader is a technical product
  manager with no depth in this domain, who reads it instead of the plan and must be able to
  accept a contract, argue with the architecture, or say the acceptance criteria do not
  describe what was asked; a document only its author's peers can read has failed. Every
  answer is in plain language: simple, terse, precise, with no jargon, and a term that cannot
  be avoided defined once, in passing, in words that reader already has. The document is six
  sections in the order What, Why, Architecture, Contracts, Acceptance criteria, Planned
  tasks, and no other heading, so no answer carries a heading of its own.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      What is being built, in one short passage.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The motivation in the user's own terms: what they said they wanted and why. Not the
      handoff, and not a restatement of what is built.
    type: text
    required: true
  architecture:
    description: >-
      The pieces and how they fit, enough for the reader to picture them. It leaves out detail
      that is easily changed later: no function names, file layout, library with many
      equivalents, or order two steps run in. It highlights the decisions that are expensive
      to reverse — a stored shape, a published interface, a boundary between pieces of code, a
      dependency on another party's release — saying what each commits to and what reversing
      it costs. Where the plan spans more than one repository it names the repository each
      piece lives in, since the planned-tasks table has no column for it; a plan inside one
      repository says nothing about repositories.
    type: text
    required: true
  contracts:
    description: >-
      The agreements the plan cuts at, one per entry, each stated so the reader could accept
      or reject it: what each party owes, and what the other may assume.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  acceptance_criteria:
    description: >-
      The document's own criteria, one per entry: what has to be true when the whole plan is
      done, as properties of the finished thing rather than the steps taken to reach it.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  planned_tasks:
    description: >-
      One entry per task of the plan, in the order the table lists them, each an object of
      four strings: `task` (its title), `delivers` (one line), `depends_on` (the titles it
      depends on, or `none`) and `location`. This table is where the document links out to
      the tasks and issues that hold the detail rather than restating any of it. Each
      `location` is the one the plan store reports for that task — a link where it lives on a
      website, a path where it is a file on this machine — and never one composed by hand.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
---
## What

{{ what }}

## Why

{{ why }}

## Architecture

{{ architecture }}

## Contracts

{% for contract in contracts %}
- {{ contract | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

## Acceptance criteria

{% for criterion in acceptance_criteria %}
- {{ criterion | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

## Planned tasks

| Task | What it delivers | Depends on | Where it lives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
{# A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. #}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\n", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{% for row in planned_tasks %}
| {{ cell(row.task) }} | {{ cell(row.delivers) }} | {{ cell(row.depends_on or "none") }} | {{ cell(row.location) }} |
{% endfor %}
